"A food caterer was told to prepare gourmet hot dogs for 36 guests. While most people would only eat one hotdog, enough was prepared by him for half of the guests to be able to have two hotdogs. However, 40 guests showed up, and everyone wanted a second hotdog. How many guests did not get a second hotdog?","A food caterer was told to prepare gourmet hot dogs for 36 guests. While most people would only eat one hotdog, he prepared enough for half of the guests to be able to have two hotdogs. However, 40 guests showed up, and everyone wanted a second hotdog. How many guests did not get a second hotdog?",26,26,26,26,"Step 1: Determine how many hot dogs were prepared.
- Planned for 36 guests, with enough extra so that half could have two.
- That means: for half of 36 (which is 18), provide an extra hot dog.
- Total hot dogs prepared = 36 (one per guest) + 18 (extras) = 54.

Step 2: Determine how many hot dogs were needed when 40 guests showed up and all wanted a second hot dog.
- Each wants two hot dogs → total needed = 40 × 2 = 80.

Step 3: Determine the shortfall in hot dogs.
- Shortfall = 80 needed − 54 prepared = 26.

Step 4: Determine how many guests did not get a second hot dog.
- Each missing second hot dog corresponds to one guest not getting a second.
- Therefore, 26 guests did not get a second hot dog.

