"Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will need to be bought by Mr. Jackson so that every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming only whole packs can be bought and some extra glue sticks are expected to be left over?","Mr. Jackson’s fourth-grade class has 27 students. He wants to give each student 2 glue sticks. The glue sticks come in packs of 8. How many packs will Mr. Jackson need to buy so every student can have 2 glue sticks, assuming he can only buy whole packs and he expects to have some extra glue sticks left over?",7,7,7,7,"Step 1: Determine total glue sticks needed.
- There are 27 students.
- Each student needs 2 glue sticks.
- Total needed = 27 × 2 = 54 glue sticks.

Step 2: Determine how many packs are required.
- Each pack contains 8 glue sticks.
- Number of packs = 54 ÷ 8 = 6.75 packs.

Step 3: Since only whole packs can be bought, round up to the next whole number.
- 6.75 rounds up to 7 packs.

Step 4: Verify extra sticks are acceptable.
- 7 packs × 8 = 56 glue sticks.
- 56 ≥ 54, with 2 extra sticks left over.

#### 7",,"Step 1: Determine total glue sticks needed.
